why 4 1/8 CROSSING The alto dips to B, a neighbor note, under the tenor's C for one eighth. The crossing is so short that both lines stay clear.
why 1 1/2 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a sixth to A♭ for a lighter iv⁶ chord. The tenor has already jumped up to C, so the two lines never get in each other's way.
why 1 3/4 LEAPS After its leap up, the bass turns back down by a fourth to E♭ for i⁶, then climbs F–G by step into the cadence. The turn is there, just not by step.
why 4 0 DOUBLING The alto falls D–C and the tenor rises B♭–C, both by step, and they meet on C, the third of the A♭ chord. Smooth lines matter more here than the textbook doubling.
why 4 0 LEAPS The bass rises D–E♭ and then leaps to A♭. The step to E♭ in between breaks up the tritone D–A♭, and the bass drops straight on to the low A♭.
why 4 1/8 LEAPS The alto runs down F–E♭–D–C–B by step and turns back up to C. A scale line sounds smooth even when its ends are a tritone apart.
why 4 1/2 LEAPS At the fermata the tenor drops to a low D, the fifth of V, to complete the chord. The pause ends the phrase, so the leap back up starts a new line.
why 5 1/2 DOUBLING Alto (F–G) and tenor (A♭–G) both move by step into the E♭ chord and land on its third, G. The soprano's climb to high E♭ shapes the chord; the doubling follows from the lines.
why 7 0 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to B♭ while the tenor steps up to the same B♭, so they meet in unison for one beat. Bach keeps the strong root motion F–B♭ in the bass.
why 7 3/4 LEAPS The tenor climbs A–B♭–C–D–E♭ by step and falls back to D. A scale like this sounds smooth even though its ends are a tritone apart.
why 11 1/2 DOUBLING The III+ chord acts like a dominant: its B in the tenor must rise to C while the alto falls D–C. Both land on C, the third of A♭, the usual doubling after a deceptive move.
